<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Danish Gold Coast</h1><p class="st-meta">1658 CE – 1850 CE · capital Christiansborg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Denmark's forts on the coast of modern Ghana, centred on Christiansborg at Osu, a hub of the Danish slave trade until the colony was sold in 1850.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Danish traders, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-carloff">Hendrik Carloff</a>, arrived on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a> in 1658 and seized the Swedish forts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> built Fort Frederiksborg near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> and in 1661 bought the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a> at Osu, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, from the king of Accra.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-west-india-and-guinea-company">Danish West India and Guinea Company</a> and later the crown ran a string of forts east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, exchanging textiles, guns and alcohol for gold and enslaved Africans, who were shipped to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danish-west-indies">Danish West Indies</a>. In 1792 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> became the first European state to ban the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, effective from 1803, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> tried plantation farming inland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Unprofitable after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, the forts were sold to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1850 and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">British Gold Coast</a> colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a>, also known as Osu Castle, later served as the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana's</a> government.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/danish-gold-coast.jpg" alt="Danish Gold Coast" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Madden and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Denmark.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1658 CE – 1850 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Christiansborg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity, Akan and Ga religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Danish, Ga</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Danish Guinea, Dansk Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=danish-gold-coast" aria-label="Danish Gold Coast on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4675 1970 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Danish Gold Coast lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4995 2226L5274 535M4995 2226L5274 535M4995 2226L4995 2226"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5274 535h0M5274 535h0M4995 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4995 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4995 2226h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=danish-gold-coast">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
